---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const title = 'Battery Capacity Testing: Ah, the C20 Rate, and How…';
const description = 'How battery capacity (Ah) is tested — the 20-hour C20 discharge, how it differs from CCA and reserve capacity, and how to read a measured capacity…';
const canonical = '/testing/battery-capacity-testing/';

const articleJsonLd = {
  '@type': 'TechArticle',
  '@id': `https://worldbatteryhub.com${canonical}#article`,
  headline: title,
  url: `https://worldbatteryhub.com${canonical}`,
  inLanguage: 'en',
  about: 'Battery capacity testing',
  articleSection: 'Battery Testing',
  dateModified: '2026-09-14',
  isPartOf: { '@id': 'https://worldbatteryhub.com/#website' },
};

const faqJsonLd = {
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: [
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'How is battery capacity tested?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'A fully-charged battery is discharged at a constant current until it reaches a cut-off voltage, and the time is recorded. Capacity (Ah) is current × time. The standard lead-acid rating is the C20 rate — a 20-hour discharge — because capacity falls at higher discharge rates.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'What is the difference between capacity testing and CCA testing?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'Capacity testing measures stored energy (a long, low-current discharge). CCA testing measures cranking power (a short, high-current cold burst). They are independent — a battery can have good CCA but degraded capacity, or vice versa.',
      },
    },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  ogType="article"
  jsonLd={[articleJsonLd, faqJsonLd]}
  author="World Battery Hub"
  dateModified="2026-09-14"
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="byline">Testing · Capacity · Last reviewed 2026-09-14</p>
      <h1>Battery Capacity Testing: Ah, the C20 Rate, and How Actual Capacity Is Measured</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Capacity is the "how much energy" number, and it is measured by draining the battery at a constant current
        and timing it. The catch — as with CCA — is that the answer depends on <strong>how fast</strong> you drain it.
      </p>

      <div class="takeaways">
        <strong>Direct answer:</strong> Capacity (Ah) is measured by discharging a fully-charged battery at a constant
        current until it reaches a cut-off voltage, then computing <strong>current × time</strong>. The standard lead-acid rating
        is the <strong>C20 rate</strong> — the current that fully drains the battery in 20 hours — because capacity drops at
        higher rates. A "60&nbsp;Ah" battery delivers 3&nbsp;A for 20 hours, but noticeably less than 60&nbsp;Ah if drained in
        5 hours.
      </div>

      <h2>The basic method</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Fully charge the battery and let it rest to a stable state.</li>
        <li>Discharge at a constant current (a resistive or electronic load).</li>
        <li>Stop at the cut-off voltage (for lead-acid, typically 10.5V for a 12V battery).</li>
        <li>Record the time; capacity = discharge current × hours.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        A 60&nbsp;Ah battery discharged at 3&nbsp;A should last 20 hours. If it only lasts 15 hours, its actual capacity is
        45&nbsp;Ah — a 25% loss from the label.
      </p>

      <h2>Why the rate matters: C20 vs higher rates</h2>
      <p>
        Capacity is <strong>not a fixed property</strong> — it falls as the discharge rate rises, because a fast discharge
        wastes more energy as internal heat and cannot extract the electrolyte deep in the plates. This is why the
        label states a rate. The C20 rate (20-hour) is the lead-acid standard; the same battery quoted at C5 (5-hour)
        or C1 (1-hour) shows a lower number. Always compare capacity at the same rate.
      </p>

      <h2>Capacity vs CCA vs reserve capacity</h2>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Test</th><th>What it measures</th><th>Method</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Capacity (C20)</strong></td><td>Total stored energy</td><td>~20 h discharge to 10.5V</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong><a href="/lead-acid/cca/how-cca-is-tested/">CCA</a></strong></td><td>Cold cranking power</td><td>30 s at −18°C to 7.2V</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Reserve capacity (RC)</strong></td><td>Minutes at a fixed load</td><td>25 A at 27°C to 10.5V</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        These are independent dimensions. A battery can hold strong CCA but degraded capacity (it cranks fine but
        runs down quickly), which is why a health check often runs both.
      </p>

      <h2>How to read a measured capacity</h2>
      <p>
        A new battery usually delivers at or slightly above its label. A reading below about <strong>80% of rated
        capacity</strong> is the conventional end-of-life signal for many applications — the battery still works, but its
        usable energy has fallen materially. For a used battery, the measured C20 capacity is the honest number to
        compare against the label.
      </p>

      <h2>What do people get wrong about capacity testing?</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>"Ah is the same at any discharge rate."</strong> No — capacity falls at higher rates, which is why the rate (C20) must be stated.</li>
        <li><strong>"Capacity testing is the same as CCA testing."</strong> They measure energy vs power — independent, not interchangeable.</li>
        <li><strong>"A battery passes if it holds 12.6V."</strong> Voltage is a state-of-charge signal, not a capacity test — a weak battery can still read full voltage.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
      <div class="takeaways">
        <ul>
          <li>Capacity (Ah) = <strong>constant-current discharge × time</strong>, to the cut-off voltage.</li>
          <li>The label rate is <strong>C20</strong> (20-hour); capacity falls at higher rates.</li>
          <li>Capacity, CCA and RC are <strong>three independent tests</strong> — energy, power and endurance.</li>
          <li>Below ~80% of rated C20 capacity is the common end-of-life threshold.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <h2>The Editor's View</h2>
      <div class="takeaways">
        <p>
          <strong>My take:</strong> The capacity label is a promise made at one specific discharge rate, and the most
          useful skill is asking "at what rate?" — because the same battery is a 60&nbsp;Ah battery at C20 and a notably
          smaller one at C5.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>What drives this:</strong> Capacity is quoted at the most flattering slow rate, which is why two "60&nbsp;Ah" batteries
          can behave differently in a real, faster-discharge application. A buyer who understands the rate — and asks
          for the measured C20 capacity on a used battery — knows what they are actually buying, rather than what
          the label once promised.
        </p>
        <p class="muted">
          <em>My editorial view, not a purchasing guarantee.</em>
        </p>
      </div>

      
      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <h3>How is battery capacity tested?</h3>
      <p>A fully-charged battery is discharged at a constant current until it reaches a cut-off voltage, and the time is recorded. Capacity (Ah) is current × time. The standard lead-acid rating is the C20 rate — a 20-hour discharge — because capacity falls at higher discharge rates.</p>
      <h3>What is the difference between capacity testing and CCA testing?</h3>
      <p>Capacity testing measures stored energy (a long, low-current discharge). CCA testing measures cranking power (a short, high-current cold burst). They are independent — a battery can have good CCA but degraded capacity, or vice versa.</p>
<h2>Sources</h2>
      <p>
        Cross-verified from battery engineering references and manufacturer test documentation. The C20 rate and
        10.5V cut-off are standard lead-acid test conventions.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>EN 50342 / JIS D5301 — capacity test methods (C20 rate).</li>
        <li>Battery Council International (BCI) — capacity and reserve-capacity definitions.</li>
        <li>Manufacturer test documentation — discharge test procedures.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="muted">Last reviewed: 2026-09-14</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
